# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit .env — only this file
# is checked in. See internal/config/config.go for parsing and defaults.

# --- Server ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Address the HTTP server listens on. Default ":1919" (all interfaces; ":1138"
# when 1919 is taken). A database created before 0.5 keeps the old ":7080"
# while this is unset, through 0.x. Setting it explicitly always wins. This
# example keeps ":7080" to match the "7080:7080" mapping in
# docker-compose.example.yml: change the two together.
KIPPLE_ADDR=:7080

# The image embeds the web app at "/". A Go build without `npm run build`
# serves the status page there instead. The status page (login, feed health,
# refresh, live events) is always at /_status.

# Directory holding the SQLite database and other persistent state.
# Default "/data" (the Dockerfile creates and owns this as the nonroot
# user; the compose file mounts a named volume here).
KIPPLE_DATA=/data

# --- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------

# Web login, used only to create the single account on the first start
# (username: 1-64 characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -). Without both, Kipple
# starts in setup mode: it prints a one-time setup code on standard error
# (`kipple setup-token` shows it again) and the browser creates the account
# (a lone KIPPLE_USERNAME is ignored). Changing them later
# has no effect: use `kipple password` to reset the password, and remove
# KIPPLE_PASSWORD from this file afterwards.
# KIPPLE_PASSWORD is empty on purpose: fill in your own, 5 to 256 characters.
# The first start refuses a password outside that range and the old example
# value "change-me".
KIPPLE_USERNAME=owner
KIPPLE_PASSWORD=

# Optional initial Google Reader API (sync client) password, 16 to 256
# characters (it guards the public sync endpoint). If unset, run
# `kipple api-password` after first start to generate and print a
# 24-character one (recommended). Applied at start only while the account has
# no Reader API password; it never replaces an existing one. A value outside
# that range stops the start that would apply it.
KIPPLE_API_PASSWORD=

# --- Cloudflare Access (optional) ----------------------------------------

# Leave both empty unless Cloudflare Access sits in front of Kipple. Kipple
# never needs Cloudflare; with both empty this feature is off and a web
# password is always required. Set both or neither: one without the other
# stops startup.
#
# When set, Kipple checks the Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion header Access adds to
# each request it lets through: the RS256 signature against the team's keys at
# https://<team domain>/cdn-cgi/access/certs (cached for an hour), the issuer
# (https://<team domain>), the audience (the AUD below) and the expiry. A
# verified token never replaces the session cookie. It does two things:
# - Settings shows the Access email you are signed in with.
# - The web password becomes optional: Settings > Remove web password (only
#   offered through Access, and it asks for the current password; the first
#   start still needs KIPPLE_PASSWORD). An account without a password signs in ONLY on requests carrying a
#   verified token, so going around Access (the LAN, a published port) cannot
#   sign in. Anyone your Access policy for this application admits can: keep
#   it to your own email. If you later unset these variables, set a password
#   again with `kipple password`.
# The Reader API (sync apps) always keeps its own API password.
#
# Team domain: Zero Trust > Settings > Custom Pages, e.g.
# yourteam.cloudflareaccess.com (https:// is optional; no path).
KIPPLE_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=
# Application Audience (AUD) tag: Zero Trust > Access > Applications > your
# Kipple application > Overview.
KIPPLE_ACCESS_AUD=

# --- Networking ---------------------------------------------------------

# Public URL Kipple is reached at. It is the "+url" part of the fetch
# User-Agent and the base of Reader API iconUrl values (when the
# greader.icon_urls setting is on). OPML exports do not use it.
# If empty, sync apps get no feed icons. It must be an absolute http(s) URL
# with a host and no query, fragment or user info, or startup stops.
# Example: https://rss.example.com
KIPPLE_PUBLIC_URL=

# Comma-separated IPs allowed to set CF-Connecting-IP and X-Forwarded-Proto
# (client IP for the login lockout and the sync login budget, which group an
# IPv6 client by its /64, and the Secure cookie
# flag). Set this to the address the reverse proxy / tunnel connects FROM as
# Kipple sees it, e.g. 192.0.2.10 (a placeholder; use your own). Leave empty
# to always use the TCP peer address. If a request carries one of those
# headers from a peer that is not listed, the header is ignored and Kipple
# logs a WARN (at most once an hour) naming the peer, so a missing entry is
# easy to spot: without it every visitor shares the proxy's IP and one
# visitor's failed logins can lock everyone out.
KIPPLE_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=

# Comma-separated extra host names Kipple answers to during first-run setup and
# while it runs without a password (open mode), e.g. rss.example.com or
# *.example.com (no scheme or port). IP addresses, localhost, single-word names
# and .local, .lan, .home.arpa, .internal and .ts.net names always work, and so
# does the host of KIPPLE_PUBLIC_URL. Other names get 421 in those two modes
# (DNS-rebinding protection); with a password they are only logged. More can be
# added in Settings (security.allowed_hosts).
KIPPLE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# --- Time -----------------------------------------------------------------

# IANA time zone. When set it is the zone for everything: log timestamps, daily
# stats and the nightly 04:10 maintenance, and the time zone in Settings is then
# shown read-only. When unset, the time zone chosen in setup or Settings is used
# (default UTC for a new install; America/New_York for one created before 0.5).
# An unknown name stops startup. time/tzdata is compiled in, so this resolves
# without a filesystem zoneinfo database.
# TZ=America/New_York

# --- Fetch scheduler -----------------------------------------------------

# How often the scheduler checks for feeds due to be polled. Default 30s,
# minimum 1s.
KIPPLE_SCHED_TICK=30s

# Number of concurrent fetch worker goroutines. Default 8.
KIPPLE_FETCH_WORKERS=8

# Max concurrent in-flight fetches per host. Default 2.
KIPPLE_FETCH_PER_HOST=2

# --- Logging ------------------------------------------------------------

# log/slog level: debug, info, warn or error. Default info.
KIPPLE_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Set to 1 to also log Google Reader API form values (truncated; the password
# and tokens are always redacted) at debug level with KIPPLE_LOG_LEVEL=debug.
# Useful for diagnosing a new sync client. Default
# unset/false.
KIPPLE_LOG_GREADER_FORMS=

# --- Go runtime -----------------------------------------------------------

# GOMEMLIMIT=64MiB   # set in the compose file, with mem_limit: 256m; a soft
#                    # Go heap limit (SQLite's own heap is outside it)
